{
  "id": 5,
  "slug": "crowd-day",
  "title": "Crowd Day",
  "concept": "Elasticity: an Auto Scaling group adds EC2 instances as a crowd arrives and removes them as it leaves, so the crowd is served and the quiet hours cost little.",
  "guidance": "hints",
  "region": "eu-central-1",
  "azs": 2,
  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "brief",
      "text": "A crowd is coming. Four Cloudlings are here now; more will walk on in groups until there are forty, then drift home again. Each keeps one kite up. A small EC2 instance has room for five kites, so the two here have room for ten. The CloudWatch weather station, bottom right, shows how busy they are: its dial is CPU. Press \"Fly kites\" and watch the dial.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "task",
      "text": "Once nineteen Cloudlings had come, there was room for only ten kites, so the rest timed out. Large instances all day would make room, but before and after the crowd they would sit almost idle, and you would pay for every one. An Auto Scaling group grows and shrinks with the crowd instead.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "place-group",
      "text": "Drag the Auto Scaling group into the band marked Mid – private, between the two EC2 instances. When that part of the sky lights up, let go.",
      "highlight": "palette:asg",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "resource-exists",
          "resourceId": "asg"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "configure",
      "text": "Click the web fleet. Keep at least two instances, so there is one in each Availability Zone; let it grow to sixteen; and choose a scaling policy that keeps average CPU near 50%.",
      "highlight": "resource:asg",
      "pulse": ["group-scaling:asg"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "group-can-scale",
          "groupId": "asg"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "attach",
      "text": "Now click the load balancer and tick the web fleet as a target. Until it is a target, no kites reach its instances, however many it has.",
      "highlight": "resource:alb",
      "pulse": ["targets:alb"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "targets-include",
          "loadBalancerId": "alb",
          "targetId": "asg"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "retire",
      "text": "The fleet replaces the two fixed EC2 instances. Click each one and choose \"Remove from the sky\" (or untick it on the load balancer). Removed, they also leave the fleet room to spread across both subnets.",
      "highlight": ["resource:app-a", "resource:app-b"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "none-of",
          "predicates": [
            {
              "kind": "targets-include",
              "loadBalancerId": "alb",
              "targetId": "app-a"
            },
            {
              "kind": "targets-include",
              "loadBalancerId": "alb",
              "targetId": "app-b"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "second-run",
      "text": "Fly the kites again. As each group arrives, watch the dial climb past your target, the bell ring, new instances launch in the fleet, and the dial fall back once they are serving.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wrap",
      "text": "Capacity followed the crowd: the fleet grew as the groups arrived and shrank as they left, so every kite was answered and the quiet hours ran on two small instances. That is elasticity. CloudWatch measured, the scaling policy decided, and the Auto Scaling group did the work.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "level-complete"
      }
    }
  ],
